{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3104","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3104","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3104\",\"parent_revision\":\"dcc9a1138a45c77f8e2d32f1bac1618c5b22d83e\",\"root_revision\":\"571ea9f99b762ab0dfd8bd7a2c798a7c06a06698bdad3011a414a471c7eb1cf3\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item \u2014 its deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict), not a mandate to ship a shipped feature.\\n\\nOrigin: mined from the golden-path task-category discussion.\\n\\nShared design constraint (same as #3097\u2013#3103): the model keeps the judgment but writes it down once, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists. No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 out of scope.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore golden-path workflow templates for known task categories** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: golden-path task-category discussion\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Why\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nEvery backlog item currently gets its plan derived from scratch by the planner, even when the task belongs to a well-known category with a stable phase skeleton. Deriving repeatedly is expensive, produces variable plan quality across sessions, and occasionally misses known-required steps. The categories in actual recurring use by the DH author: (1) adding, extending, or evaluating capabilities of an AI harness plugin; (2) adding a new feature to an existing development project; (3) starting a new development project / new-project setup with a known or unknown stack, including best-practice checklists ensuring the key project items are created and set up \u2014 git, linters, formatters, config, project layout, services, commit hooks, CI/CD, unit/integration/e2e test system stubs, PR templates, licensing, READMEs; (4) designing, extending, or implementing a knowledge base or documentation system; (5) article writing for human readers; (6) creating standalone skills for harnesses for release and documentation, solving single issues in their own repo; (7) creating hooks or plugins that require cross-harness capabilities \u2014 including what to check for in what the skill does and its audience, where to find the official docs, and known gotchas per harness.\\n\\nA golden path flips planning for a recognized category from derive-from-scratch to select-a-pre-validated-template-and-specialize. DH already contains the embryo: the sdlc-layers system (Layer 1 language manifests, Layer 2 stack profiles such as python-cli and python-fastapi) is a golden path for exactly one category (project setup) keyed only by stack. This item generalizes it along an orthogonal axis: task category x stack profile, composed at planning time (\\\"new project setup\\\" template + python-fastapi profile = concrete plan skeleton).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nEvery backlog item currently gets its plan derived from scratch by the planner, even when the task belongs to a well-known category with a stable phase skeleton. Deriving repeatedly is expensive, produces variable plan quality across sessions, and occasionally misses known-required steps. The categories in actual recurring use by the DH author: (1) adding, extending, or evaluating capabilities of an AI harness plugin; (2) adding a new feature to an existing development project; (3) starting a new development project / new-project setup with a known or unknown stack, including best-practice checklists ensuring the key project items are created and set up \u2014 git, linters, formatters, config, project layout, services, commit hooks, CI/CD, unit/integration/e2e test system stubs, PR templates, licensing, READMEs; (4) designing, extending, or implementing a knowledge base or documentation system; (5) article writing for human readers; (6) creating standalone skills for harnesses for release and documentation, solving single issues in their own repo; (7) creating hooks or plugins that require cross-harness capabilities \u2014 including what to check for in what the skill does and its audience, where to find the official docs, and known gotchas per harness.\\n\\nA golden path flips planning for a recognized category from derive-from-scratch to select-a-pre-validated-template-and-specialize. DH already contains the embryo: the sdlc-layers system (Layer 1 language manifests, Layer 2 stack profiles such as python-cli and python-fastapi) is a golden path for exactly one category (project setup) keyed only by stack. This item generalizes it along an orthogonal axis: task category x stack profile, composed at planning time (\\\"new project setup\\\" template + python-fastapi profile = concrete plan skeleton).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Mitigates\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRepeated plan-derivation cost for known task shapes; inconsistent plan quality across sessions; missing setup/checklist steps in category work; partially, the unvetted skill-injection channel (a template can PIN the curated skills/profiles its category needs, demoting open-ended runtime discovery to a gap-filler \u2014 see the companion item \\\"Explore measuring and vetting the skill/agent discovery pass-through to task-workers\\\" (#3105)).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefit Lands In\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nPlanning cost and consistency; setup completeness (checklists become mechanically verifiable); trust tiering of injected content.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nPlanning cost and consistency; setup completeness (checklists become mechanically verifiable); trust tiering of injected content.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Proposed Exploration\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n1. Define the category taxonomy from the list above, with one open design question to resolve first \u2014 whether \\\"article writing\\\" and \\\"knowledge base / documentation system\\\" are one authoring category with two audiences or two categories.\\n2. Define the template format: phase skeleton + category checklist expressed as structured acceptance criteria with check_commands (so the existing T0-baseline / TN-verification machinery enforces completeness mechanically, no agent judgment about \\\"is the project set up\\\") + pinned skill/profile set for the category.\\n3. Category detection at groom time via the existing dh:classifier mechanism (a second taxonomy table), falling back to today's derive-from-scratch when nothing matches.\\n4. Golden path means default-on, easy-off \u2014 the template is the starting point the planner edits with stated reasons, never rails.\\n5. Prototype ONE template end-to-end (new-project-setup is the best candidate \u2014 richest checklist, existing Layer 2 composition) and run it on a real or sandbox project, comparing plan quality and missed-step count against a derive-from-scratch baseline.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n1. Define the category taxonomy from the list above, with one open design question to resolve first \u2014 whether \\\"article writing\\\" and \\\"knowledge base / documentation system\\\" are one authoring category with two audiences or two categories.\\n2. Define the template format: phase skeleton + category checklist expressed as structured acceptance criteria with check_commands (so the existing T0-baseline / TN-verification machinery enforces completeness mechanically, no agent judgment about \\\"is the project set up\\\") + pinned skill/profile set for the category.\\n3. Category detection at groom time via the existing dh:classifier mechanism (a second taxonomy table), falling back to today's derive-from-scratch when nothing matches.\\n4. Golden path means default-on, easy-off \u2014 the template is the starting point the planner edits with stated reasons, never rails.\\n5. Prototype ONE template end-to-end (new-project-setup is the best candidate \u2014 richest checklist, existing Layer 2 composition) and run it on a real or sandbox project, comparing plan quality and missed-step count against a derive-from-scratch baseline.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nsdlc-layers Layer 1/2 (existing embryo); #3097 (a template can carry default complexity scores per phase); companion item \\\"Explore measuring and vetting the skill/agent discovery pass-through to task-workers\\\" (#3105) (templates supply the curated tier).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"de4a5cfa330b5f9389070ad988e003f5465bfd8ea6e4f8a0006d9293442bb05f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIqUjg\"}"}